#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
OpenGEO corpus -- keyword-stuffing intervention, v2 (kwstuff-v2).

Redesign of kwstuff-v1 (corpus_kwstuff_v1.json), which came back an uninformative
null (H6 pooled delta -0.001, p=0.57) because its "control" arm reused corpus v0.4's
`treatment` variant wholesale -- text already proven to sit at ~98.5% pooled CPR.
There was no headroom left for keyword stuffing to move citation in either direction;
5 of 8 models had zero variance in that cell. See
`preregistrations/2026-08-kwstuff-v2.md` for the full diagnosis and the redesign this
corpus implements.

Where v1 mistakenly reused a ceiling-adjacent baseline, v2 builds a genuinely
mid-range one:

    moderate = a NEW baseline, built by recombining two pieces already validated in
               corpus_v0.4.json for this same prompt: the sentence(s) stating ONE of
               the question's two target facts, taken verbatim from v0.4's
               `treatment` variant (so it is genuinely specific about that one fact),
               plus orthogonal filler content taken verbatim from v0.4's `control`
               variant (so it says nothing, specific or vague, about the OTHER fact).
               This deliberately keeps CPR off both the floor (v0.2's ceiling-null
               problem in reverse) and the ceiling (v1's problem) -- it answers real
               specific
               facts, but only half of what the question asks.
    stuffed  = the same `moderate` facts, with the prompt's core topic phrase
               (identical keyword mapping to v1) awkwardly repeated 4-8 times where
               natural writing wouldn't -- the same textbook keyword-stuffing pattern
               as v1, unchanged, since the intervention under test (H6) is unchanged.
               Length-matched to `moderate` within +/-3 words, same discipline as
               every corpus version in this project.

Both variants are recombinations of text already validated against real models in
corpus_v0.4.json -- no brand-new prose was authored from scratch, to minimize the risk
of reintroducing an undetected ceiling or keyword/directional-echo problem. This is
lower-risk than v1's assumption (which also relied on "already validated" text, but
reused it *wholesale* rather than *recombined*, and was wrong) -- see the
pre-registration for the real-model spot-check this project committed to running
before the full round, specifically because that v1 assumption failed once already.

As with v1: reuses corpus/build_corpus.py's 48 prompts wholesale -- same questions,
same domains, same 5 non-target distractor documents per prompt, same target format
balance (8 prompts/format). Only the target document's two variants differ.
"""
import json, pathlib, hashlib
import build_corpus as base

